eed for more rapid testing but in in  good combination with the the ptr based  base test and i think you know great  discussion on preparedness  uh how do we have roads broader range of  assay may be  ready available but also challenges that  goes with any pandemic of that  of that sort um any what i'd like is  maybe go back and do another  final lightning round for anything that  uh you'd like  anyone would like to add uh any further  learning anything we have not covered  uh for for the for the future so maybe  i'll go back to you uh  dave we'll do it in the same order david  cepheus any any final thought  yeah i i think that you know getting  back to the point of getting ready for  the future  it's going to be increasingly important  to develop  sort of universal diagnostics capacity  that can be adapted quickly  to a new pandemic threat and with  nucleic acid testing  we can do that we can actually stockpile  universal cartridges and universal  reagents and adapt those quickly  to the uh to the new targets  um and i think that will really help us  be able to respond more quickly  um but we have to think creatively about  how we do this  and um and what we need to get ready for  dave gene drive um  i think i think for for me the lasting  changes are going to be just around the  recognition  of the diagnostics industry you know  within government  and finance business general public i  mean  my sort of local social media groups  you know people talk about pcr testing  and you know  who whoever would have thought that ever  would have happened you know  um but there's you know clearly a much  higher level of interest of investment  um a focus on on the industry and the  industry players  and i think that can't be anything but  positive  david yeah nothing  new really to add to that um i'll just  finish where i started i think  um the fact that diagnostic testing the  value of diagnostic testing is now  acknowledged as a as a tool  um a critical tool for tackling these  global  crises and also the recognition  of the strong industry national industry  as a key strategic asset  i mean the uk government actually said  that one of the reasons they felt that  they were slower than some countries to  respond was because they had a weaker  diagnostics industry and they've they've  explicitly stated that they want that  to change and that what will come out of  this will be a stronger  diagnostics industry um so i think that  will be a very positive and enduring uh  legacy  uh from this and also to reiterate what  dave dave bud said um it's just  it's just a unique time when you pick up  national newspapers and  listen to the national news on tv and  people are talking about test  specificity and test sensitivity and  predictive values and it's you know it's  a it's never happened before  so i think we've i think the industries  finally come out of the shadows of the  other  uh healthcare sectors and uh and and  should hopefully uh benefit from  recognition of the value that it really  does deliver  talking about testing at the pub in the  evening right yeah yeah yeah  that's a new thing right exactly yeah  brandon final thoughts please  between cpr and pcr  um but you know i i i think with that i  suppose with with that  um new focus i suppose on diagnostics  as a as a thing of importance um  you know i hope out of all of this  there's the the there will be  some degree of of patience learned uh  and  that you know in in the in the responses  both from government and from industry  that um you know people follow the  science  you know and and i think going back to  the question on sequential testing  um you know it's a good it's a good  thing to stop and examine the use cases  relative to the capabilities of these  tests right and you know  for for antigen tests you know antigen  tests no matter how sensitive they are  are still much more likely to give a  false positive in a low intensity  environment then give a correct  answer ri